### The C++ result

The C++ program built and passed every functional and boundary check in both steps: {{cpp_f1_pass}} of {{cpp_f1}} functional and {{cpp_b1_pass}} of {{cpp_b1}} boundary checks in the first step, {{cpp_f2_pass}} of {{cpp_f2}} and {{cpp_b2_pass}} of {{cpp_b2}} in the second.

What failed was one security check. The program the AI wrote served the files of its own folder. When the judge asked for `{{cpp_request}}`, the build file that says how this program is built came back as it is. A server written this way can hand out its source code or its settings the same way, so it counts as a vulnerability. The program passed {{cpp_s1_pass}} of {{cpp_s1}} security checks in the first step and {{cpp_s2_pass}} of {{cpp_s2}} in the second. Each step counted {{cpp_vulns1}} vulnerability, and in the second step the same check still failed, which also counted as {{cpp_broken2}} check of the earlier step still broken after the change.

This is the AI's result, not a fault of the judge. At the start the C++ folder held only that one build file, so serving files came from the code the AI wrote. In the other {{n_full}} languages, {{other_failed_checks}} checks failed.

With one run per language, this does not show that code written in C++ is less safe. It does show that the security checks catch a problem the functional checks alone do not see.

## What one run used

### Measured: time and tokens

- **AI working time**: {{agent_seconds_mean}} seconds per run on average (shortest {{agent_seconds_min}}, longest {{agent_seconds_max}}). The {{n_runs}} runs add up to {{agent_minutes_total}} minutes.
- **Whole run** (preparing the containers, the AI's work, the build and the judging together): {{wall_seconds_mean}} seconds per run on average (between {{wall_seconds_min}} and {{wall_seconds_max}}). The {{n_runs}} runs add up to {{wall_minutes_total}} minutes.
- **Judging time**: {{judge_seconds_mean}} seconds per run on average (both steps together).
- **Tokens** (the {{n_runs}} runs together): input {{tokens_input}}, cache read {{tokens_cache_read}}, cache write {{tokens_cache_creation}}, output {{tokens_output}}. There were {{turns_total}} turns in all.
- **The amount the tool writes down**: {{cost_total}} dollars for the {{n_runs}} runs together. The tool computes this figure by itself; the runs were on a subscription, so it is not money that was charged. Of the limit notices, {{safety.limit_events_using_extra_usage}} said that extra paid usage was in use.

### The weekly limit: only an upper bound

While it runs, the tool reports the weekly utilization in whole percent. In the {{weekly.later_runs}} later runs it did so {{weekly.readings_per_step}} time in each step, early in the step.

- Over the {{weekly.later_runs}} runs from TypeScript to C++, the first reading was {{weekly.later_first_reading}}% and the last reading was {{weekly.later_last_reading}}%. It moved by {{weekly.moved_between_the_two_readings_points}} percentage points.
- The two readings do not bracket all of the {{weekly.later_runs}} runs. The first reading came in the first step of TypeScript, after {{weekly.first_reading_after_replies}} of the model's {{weekly.first_reading_step_replies}} replies. The last reading came in the second step of C++, after {{weekly.last_reading_after_replies}} of {{weekly.last_reading_step_replies}} replies: during the sixth run, not after it ended. What lies wholly between the two readings is {{weekly.steps_wholly_between_the_readings}} of the {{weekly.steps_of_the_later_runs}} steps, {{weekly.agent_seconds_wholly_between_the_readings}} seconds of AI working time (all {{weekly.later_runs}} runs together are {{weekly.agent_seconds_of_the_later_runs}} seconds).
- Of the {{n_runs}} counted runs, {{weekly.runs_whose_readings_moved_between_first_and_after}} had a first reading that differed from its last.
- The earlier rise of {{weekly.rise_between_the_batches_points}} percentage points, from {{weekly.later_stored_before}}% to {{weekly.later_first_reading}}%, is not what runs used. The {{weekly.later_stored_before}}% is a reading of the counted Python run, and the {{weekly.later_first_reading}}% is the first reading of the TypeScript run about {{weekly.hours_between_first_and_later_runs}} hours later. No measurement ran in between; the same account was used for development work. The same holds for the {{weekly.first_batch_before}}% and {{weekly.first_batch_first}}% of the counted Python run: the {{weekly.first_batch_before}}% is the reading at the end of the Python run before it that is not counted (started {{e2.started_hhmm_utc}}).

**What this bounds.** The figure is shown in whole percent, so the {{weekly.agent_seconds_wholly_between_the_readings}} seconds between the two readings used less than {{weekly.display_step_points}} percentage point. In terms of the average run ({{agent_seconds_mean}} seconds) that is less than {{weekly.one_run_below_points}} percentage point per run ({{weekly.display_step_points}} × {{agent_seconds_mean}} ÷ {{weekly.agent_seconds_wholly_between_the_readings}}).

**What it cannot tell.** There is no lower bound. It cannot tell whether one run uses close to that upper bound or a hundredth of it. The upper bound times {{plan.runs}} runs is {{weekly.baseline_below_points}} percentage points, so this number cannot even tell whether the whole baseline measurement fits inside one week's limit. The runner stops at {{weekly.runner_stops_at_percent}}% weekly and the last reading was {{weekly.later_last_reading}}%, which leaves {{weekly.room_to_the_stop_points}} percentage points. In the worst case ({{weekly.one_run_below_points}} percentage point per run) that room is used up after {{weekly.runs_in_that_room_at_the_bound}} runs ({{weekly.room_to_the_stop_points}} ÷ {{weekly.one_run_below_points}}). In fact far more runs may fit.

**What further measurement would settle it.** That is worked out below, under "What the next measurements can and cannot settle".

### The {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit: the tool does not report it

Of the {{n_runs}} runs, {{five_hour.runs_that_reported_it}} reported the utilization of the {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit. So this limit was kept only by the runner's own guard. The guard adds up the working time of the agents the runner ran and starts no new run once it reaches {{five_hour.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes within {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}} hours. After the {{weekly.later_runs}} runs from TypeScript to C++ the guard had counted {{five_hour.guard_minutes_after_later_runs}} minutes. After the Python run it had counted {{five_hour.guard_minutes_after_first_batch}} minutes; the runner was then changed and the guard keeps its record in a new place, so the TypeScript run started from {{five_hour.guard_minutes_before_later_runs}} minutes.

**What this bounds.** Only that {{five_hour.guard_minutes_after_later_runs}} of the {{five_hour.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes the runner set for itself were used.

**What it cannot tell.** How much of the account's real {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit was used. The guard's {{five_hour.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes are not a measured value; they were chosen to be careful. The guard does not count other work done on the same account.

**What further measurement would settle it.** As long as the tool does not report this value, run records alone cannot tell. The cheapest way is to read it from the account's usage screen before and after a row of runs and write it down. Only the account's owner can see that screen.

## Safety

- **Was the token seen on a command line in the {{safety.watched_runs}} watched runs?** While the runs went on, the process table of this machine was read every {{safety.reading_interval_seconds}} seconds: {{safety.readings}} readings in all, and {{safety.readings_with_the_token}} of them showed the token. Of the {{safety.readings}} readings, {{safety.readings_later_runs}} belong to the {{weekly.later_runs}} runs made after the runner was last changed, and {{safety.readings_while_the_agent_worked}} were taken while the AI worked. {{safety.runs_also_watched_after_their_end}} runs were also watched after they had ended.
- **The {{safety.starts_without_a_watch}} starts that were not watched**: the Python starts at {{e1.started_hhmm_utc}} and {{e2.started_hhmm_utc}} had no watch of the process table. The run tool they used was Harbor as it is released, and that version passes the login on a command line when it starts the AI. An automatic check in the repository confirms this behaviour with a made-up secret. The change that prevents it first entered the repository at {{day.fix_first_committed_hhmm_utc}}, after both starts ({{e1.minutes_before_the_fix_was_first_committed}} minutes after the start at {{e1.started_hhmm_utc}}, {{e2.minutes_before_the_fix_was_first_committed}} minutes after the start at {{e2.started_hhmm_utc}}), and was merged at {{day.fix_merged_hhmm_utc}}. Of the {{n_runs}} counted runs, {{day.counted_runs_started_before_the_fix_was_merged}} (Python) started before the merge and {{day.counted_runs_started_after_the_fix_was_merged}} after it; all of them were watched.
  - Known: while those {{safety.starts_without_a_watch}} starts were starting the AI, the token will have been visible in the process table of this machine, which other programs running on the machine can read. It was not seen directly, because nothing watched.
  - Not known: whether anything did read it in that time, and for how many seconds it was visible.
  - This exposure is what the change merged later that day was for.
- **Was the token left in the results?** Every file the runs left was searched for the token. Counting the starts that are not counted, {{safety.clean_leak_scans}} of {{safety.leak_scans}} scans were clean and {{safety.leak_hits}} places were found. Whether there were files that could not be read, and so could not be checked, is recorded only for the {{safety.runs_whose_scan_says_what_it_could_not_read}} counted runs; there it is {{safety.files_the_scan_could_not_read}} files.
- **What was left**: containers left are recorded only for the {{safety.runs_that_recorded_containers}} counted runs; there it is {{safety.containers_left}}. The watch stopped a run midway {{safety.runs_where_harbor_was_stopped}} times. Problems found in the judging environment: {{safety.judge_environment_problems}}. Errors of the AI tool: {{safety.agent_errors}}.
- **The limit of this check**: the watch of the process table sees command lines only. The environment variables of running processes were not looked at. With readings {{safety.reading_interval_seconds}} seconds apart, a process that came and went faster can be missed.

## The limits of this pilot

- **One run per language.** We do not know how far the score and the time spread when the same run is repeated. Only Python ran to the end twice that day, and one of the two is counted.
- **One task.** Other tasks may take longer or shorter.
- **One model, and one that was not chosen.** The tool is what was pinned; the tool picked the model ({{model}}). Larger models were not measured.
- **One company's model.** Numbers measured on one company's models alone do not fix the core design of the language.
- **The limits.** Only an upper bound is known for the weekly limit, and nothing for the {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit.
- **Nothing was emulated.** The numbers come from real runs. Their weakness is the small sample.

## How long the first baseline measurement takes

The charter plans the first baseline measurement as {{plan.runs}} runs: {{plan.models}} AI models write {{plan.tasks}} tasks in the {{plan.languages}} existing languages {{plan.repeats}} times each ({{plan.models}} × {{plan.tasks}} × {{plan.languages}} × {{plan.repeats}} = {{plan.runs}}). The table that comes out of it is the bar Dodam must beat.

Assumptions:

- One machine, this one, one run at a time.
- Runs at night only. A night is {{plan.night_hours}} hours, and every night is used.
- The runner's guard stays as it is ({{plan.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes within {{plan.guard_window_hours}} hours). A new run starts only while the counted time is below the limit.
- The AI working time and the whole time of a run are the averages measured here ({{agent_seconds_mean}} and {{wall_seconds_mean}} seconds). Where the AI working time is taken as a multiple, only the added working time is added to the whole time; judging and preparing the containers are taken to stay the same.
- The subscription's weekly limit and its real {{plan.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit are taken not to stop anything. As said above, this has not been confirmed.

As measured:

- At {{x1.agent_minutes_per_run}} minutes per run the guard lets {{x1.starts_per_window}} runs start in one {{plan.guard_window_hours}}-hour window. The last of them starts because the time counted until then is just below {{plan.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes. These {{x1.starts_per_window}} runs are over in {{x1.minutes_for_those_starts}} minutes.
- Once the first runs leave the {{plan.guard_window_hours}}-hour window, the same number can run again, and this second row also ends inside the {{plan.night_hours}} hours. That makes {{x1.runs_per_night}} runs a night.
- {{plan.runs}} runs divided by {{x1.runs_per_night}} a night, rounded up, is {{x1.nights}} nights.
- The AI works {{x1.agent_hours_total}} hours in all. In the unit of the schedule simulation, the AI working day (what one agent does in {{plan.agent_hours_per_working_day}} hours), that is {{x1.working_days}} working days. The plan had taken {{plan.plan_hours_per_run}} hours per run and set aside {{plan_working_days}} working days.

| AI working time of one run | Minutes | Starts in one window | Runs a night | Nights | AI working time in all (hours) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| As measured | {{x1.agent_minutes_per_run}} | {{x1.starts_per_window}} | {{x1.runs_per_night}} | {{x1.nights}} | {{x1.agent_hours_total}} |
| If {{x2.factor}} times the measured value (an assumption) | {{x2.agent_minutes_per_run}} | {{x2.starts_per_window}} | {{x2.runs_per_night}} | {{x2.nights}} | {{x2.agent_hours_total}} |
| If {{x4.factor}} times the measured value (an assumption) | {{x4.agent_minutes_per_run}} | {{x4.starts_per_window}} | {{x4.runs_per_night}} | {{x4.nights}} | {{x4.agent_hours_total}} |

The charter gives this measurement the {{row_nights}} nights from {{row_start_long}} to {{row_end_long}}. To end inside them it needs {{row_runs_per_night}} runs a night, and for that the AI working time of a run must not exceed {{largest_minutes}} minutes on average ({{largest_factor}} times the measured value).

**Not measured.** One of the {{plan.models}} models has been measured, and one of the {{plan.tasks}} tasks. How much longer a larger model works is known only once it is measured. The {{x2.factor}} times and {{x4.factor}} times in the table are assumptions, not measurements.

### What the next measurements can and cannot settle

All of this is worked out with the runner's guard as it is now.

**First night: the same task with the same model, as many runs as the guard lets one night hold.** That is {{next.same.runs}} runs ({{next.same.whole_rounds_of_the_languages}} rounds of the {{plan.languages}} languages and {{next.same.runs_beyond_whole_rounds}} more). The AI works about {{next.same.agent_minutes}} minutes.

- The weekly limit. A step reports once, early, so {{next.same.runs_wholly_between_first_and_last_reading}} runs lie wholly between the first and the last reading of the night. If the figure does not move between them, one run uses less than {{next.same.one_run_below_points_if_unmoved}} percentage point ({{weekly.display_step_points}} ÷ {{next.same.runs_wholly_between_first_and_last_reading}}) and {{plan.runs}} runs use less than {{next.same.baseline_below_points_if_unmoved}} percentage points. That is inside the {{next.same.room_on_the_pilot_day_points}} percentage points of room that were left on the day of the pilot. If the figure moves by one step, {{plan.runs}} runs may use up to {{next.same.baseline_points_if_moved_one_step_at_most}} percentage points, and whether they fit that room stays open. Other work on the same account can only raise the figure, so "it did not move" can be trusted, while "it moved" may be that other work.
- The spread. Each language is repeated more than {{next.same.whole_rounds_of_the_languages}} times, which shows how far the score and the time of a repeated run spread.
- What stays open. {{next.same.models_left}} models and {{next.same.tasks_left}} tasks are still unmeasured. How much room there is on that day depends on how much the account was used that week.

**Second night: the largest model, once per language, {{next.large.runs}} runs.**

- Does it fit the guard? If that model's AI working time is up to {{next.large.all_start_in_one_window_up_to_factor}} times the measured value ({{next.large.agent_minutes_at_that_factor}} minutes per run), all {{next.large.runs}} runs start in one window. If it is longer, the remaining runs wait until the window opens again, and up to {{next.large.all_end_in_one_night_up_to_factor}} times ({{next.large.agent_minutes_at_the_night_factor}} minutes per run) they still end within one night. At {{x4.factor}} times, for example, {{next.large.starts_in_the_first_window_at_factor_4}} runs start in the first window and {{next.large.runs_in_the_night_at_factor_4}} end within the night. Beyond that, one more night is used.
- What it settles. How many times the measured value that model works on this task. Every run that ends gives one value.
- What stays open. {{next.large.models_left}} model, {{next.large.tasks_left}} tasks, and the share of the weekly limit a larger model takes: with {{next.large.runs}} runs an unmoved figure only says less than {{next.large.one_run_below_points_if_unmoved}} percentage point per run.

So these two nights do not settle which row of the table above holds. A row of the table is an average over {{plan.models}} models and {{plan.tasks}} tasks; the two nights measure {{next.large.models_covered_with_the_first_night}} models and {{next.large.tasks_covered}} task. What they give is a number for one part of that average.

**A way to measure without any new run (a separate piece of work; it is not built here).** The Claude Code session records on this machine hold the tokens used, the model and the time. Adding up from those records the tokens used between two readings of the weekly utilization gives what one percentage point costs in tokens, and from that what a week gives. The day of the pilot already has two such readings, {{weekly.later_stored_before}}% and {{weekly.later_first_reading}}%.

- What it settles. What one account gives in a week, which is the capacity assumption of the schedule simulation.
- What stays open. What was used on that account outside this machine is not in the records. Tokens of different models may weigh differently. The {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit cannot be found this way either.

No new run was made while this document was written.

## Judging the {{row_month}} schedule again

The pilot changes the inputs of the schedule simulation in two ways. They are run apart to show the part of each.

- **Finished work is counted as finished.** The charter had planned the pilot from {{pilot_row_start_short}} to {{pilot_row_end_short}}; it ended on {{date_short}}, {{pilot_days_early}} days before the day it was to start. That the pilot ran also means the work before it is finished (the task format, the sample task, the runner's skeleton). These are entered as finished on {{date_short}}.
- **The pilot's numbers are entered.** The plan had {{plan_working_days}} working days of AI time for the baseline measurement. As measured it is {{x1.working_days}} working days; {{x2.working_days}} if the AI working time is {{x2.factor}} times, {{x4.working_days}} if it is {{x4.factor}} times. And because of the guard and the night hours the baseline measurement cannot take fewer than {{x1.nights}} nights ({{x2.nights}} nights at {{x2.factor}} times, {{x4.nights}} nights at {{x4.factor}} times).

One input did not change: the AI working days the subscriptions give in a week. The pilot was meant to measure it, but the weekly utilization did not move, so it could not. The capacity was therefore run three ways.

- **The plan's capacity**: one account per AI company, about {{planning.days_a_week}} working days a week. This is the condition the charter's schedule stands on.
- **Today's conditions**: one Claude account, shared with the owner's other work. The charter counts this as about {{claude_shared.days_a_week}} working days a week.
- **One Claude account used for Dodam alone**: counted as about {{claude_dedicated.days_a_week}} working days a week.

What one account gives in a week (the {{claude_shared.days_a_week}} and the {{claude_dedicated.days_a_week}}) is an assumption, not a measured value.

The schedule simulation rolls the tasks of the work breakdown forward week by week, in the order of what must come first. Tasks that run in the same week share the subscription capacity, tasks run over their planned length, and risks strike. {{tasks_rolled}} tasks were rolled (the {{tasks_left_out}} measuring task added after the forecast was made is left out). Each case was run {{sims}} times with each of {{n_seeds}} seeds, {{runs_per_case}} runs in all. "Middle" means half of those runs had ended by that day, and "{{pct80}}%" means {{pct80}}% of them had. The simulation does not know how far the {{plan.tasks}} tasks and the measuring tool have come by now.

**How precise the figures are.** Results come in steps of a week. Taking each seed of a case by itself, the middle dates differed by up to {{noise.middle_dates_move_up_to_days}} days, the {{pct80}}% dates by up to {{noise.upper_dates_move_up_to_days}} days and the chances by up to {{noise.chances_move_up_to_points}} percentage points. The dates and chances below are to be read with that width. The brackets beside a chance give the range over the seeds taken one by one.

